@NODE system "Overview - System Requirements"
@TOC contents
@{" 1     Overview                      " LINK intro}
@{" 1.1   Introduction                  " LINK intro}
@{" 1.2   AmiGUS mini Key Features      " LINK features}
@{" 1.3   Hardware Overview             " LINK overview}
@{" 1.3.1 Hardware Ports                " LINK ports}
@{" 1.3.2 Hardware Components           " LINK components}
@{" 1.4   System Requirements           " LINK system}

In order to make full use of AmiGUS features, the following range of
system configurations is recommended:

@{" 1.4.1 Minimum Configuration         " LINK system}

       - Amiga 600 computer
       - 1 MB Chip
       - 68000 CPU, 7 MHz
       - Kickstart 2.05

@{" 1.4.2 Recommended Configuration     " LINK system}

       - Amiga 1200 computer
       - 2 MB Chip, 4 MB Fast RAM
       - 68030 CPU, 25 MHz
       - Kickstart 3.x

Even though we ensure that the most essential tools required to configure
the card do work on all revisions of the Amiga operating system, using
Kick 3.x is strongly recommended to yield maximum application
compatibility.

@{" 2     Installation                  " LINK hw_inst}
@ENDNODE

@NODE hw_inst "Installation - Card Installation"
@TOC contents
@{" 1     Overview                      " LINK intro}
@{" 2     Installation                  " LINK hw_inst}
@{" 2.1   Card Installation             " LINK hw_inst}

Turn off your Amiga and insert the AmiGUS mini card into the PCMCIA port
of your Amiga 600/1200, located on the left-hand side of the computer.
Make sure there is enough room to accommodate the additional space needed
for the card itself and the cables.

@{"AmiGUS mini Setup" system "MultiView ilbm/card12.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/card12.iff}

The AmiGUS mini card provides both a line-level RCA stereo output and
a mini TOSLINK optical output on the same 3.5 mm jack to connect to
standard household analogue or digital stereo receiver equipment.

The TOSLINK output is very flexible and supports multiple sampling rates
accommodating your output devices capabilities. The default output
sampling rate is 48 kHz, which should be compatible with all equipment
providing a TOSLINK interface. Output rate can be adjusted in the mixer
settings.

The analogue input can be used to loop-through the Amiga's external
audio output by using a Cinch-to-3.5 mm adaptor cable, or to connect
any other analogue sound source which supports line-level audio
output.

@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK sw_inst20}

@ENDNODE

@NODE sw_inst20 "Installation - Software Installation"
@TOC contents
@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1   Installation                  " LINK sw_inst20}

The AmiGUS mini software comes in the following packages:
- As an LHA archive for OS 2.x/3.x
- As sets of ADF files for OS 2.x/3.x including one Manual Disk

@{" 3.1.1 OS 2.0+ Software Installation " LINK sw_inst20}

On OS 2.0 and later, the AmiGUS software installation relies on the
Amiga Installer being available in path from the underlying OS.

But Commodore only introduced the Installer in OS 2.1 and since then,
it is part of the AmigaOS installation. So before installing the
AmiGUS software on OS 2.0, you may need to install the Installer from:

https://aminet.net/util/misc/Installer-43_3.lha

We tested the installation with an early Installer from OS 2.1 that does
not even have version information included, but there may be even older
or worse versions of it out there. In case you experience issues during
AmiGUS software installation, Installer 43.3 as per above may serve as a
common ground.

Similarly, to use any AmigaGuide on AmigaOS versions earlier than 3.0,
namely OS 2.0 and OS 2.1, you will need to install
the "AmigaGuide Development" version from:

https://aminet.net/text/hyper/aguide34.lha

The AmiGUS AmigaGuide manual looks best on OS 3.2 and above, but you
can at least read it conveniently using an older version.

Again, the installation comes in multiple languages and should just work
the same in all languages. If you would like to see the installation
procedure and/or the manual translated to other languages, feel free to
reach out to us e.g. via our GitHub presence.

"Novice User", "Intermediate User" and "Expert User" level installations
are available.

"Novice User" installs all recommended parts without further questions
to the recommended destinations. This should work fine for most users.

Please note:
- If you downgrade your CPU after installation and experience issues
  running any AmiGUS software, you may need to reinstall the drivers.
- The default installation path of AmiGUS tools and manual is
  Sys:Expansion/AmiGUS - which may collide with some versions of
  Commodore's Janus bridgeboard software. If you experience any issues,
  please delete the AmiGUS software installed to that location, reinstall
  and select another path.

In "Intermediate User" level installations, you may pick and choose a lot
of options. For the details of those, please refer to the help texts
embedded into the Installer when pushing the "Help" buttons available
everywhere.

In addition to the above, only "Expert User" level installations allow
for placement of all CPU or debug versions of AHI and MHI drivers next
to the activated ones.

Please keep in mind that using AHI and MHI requires additional software.
Refer to sections @{" 5 AHI Driver Framework " LINK ahi} and @{"5 MHI Driver Framework" LINK mhi}
for a detailed description.

@{" 3.2   Mixer Tool                    " LINK mixer1}
@ENDNODE

@NODE mixer1 "Software Tools - Mixer Tool"
@TOC contents
@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1   Installation                  " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1.1 OS 2.0+ Software Installation " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.2   Mixer Tool                    " LINK mixer1}

The AmiGUS Mixer Tool provides individual volume control over the various
sound sources that are processed and mixed by the digital sound processor.

@{"Mixer" system "MultiView ilbm/mix10.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/mix10.iff}
Across the panels, you can adjust the volume using the @{b}Left / Right@{ub} volume
sliders for channel-independent control. When the @{b}Lock@{ub} button is enabled,
Left /Right are always set to equal values when the sliders are changed.

By using the @{b}Mix@{ub} slider, it is possible to cross-mix the stereo audio
signal, thus lessening the amount of channel separation.

@{" 3.2.1 Mixer Panel Descriptions      " LINK mixer2}
@{" 3.3   FPGA Flash Tool               " LINK flash1}
@{" 3.3.1 Available Options             " LINK flash2}

@ENDNODE

@NODE mixer2 "Software Tools - Mixer Panel Descriptions"
@TOC contents
@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1   Installation                  " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1.1 OS 2.0+ Software Installation " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.2   Mixer Tool                    " LINK mixer1}
@{" 3.2.1 Mixer Panel Descriptions      " LINK mixer2}

Below you will find a description of each panel's functionality and
configuration options.

+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| Panel            | Description                                         |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| AHI Sound        | This panel provides volume control over all sounds  |
|                  | which are played back using the AHI driver          |
|                  | framework.                                          |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| MHI Sound        | This panel provides volume control over all sounds  |
|                  | which are played back using the MHI driver          |
|                  | framework, output by the VS1063 audio codec.        |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| Wave Sound       | This panel provides volume control over all sounds  |
|                  | which are played using the 32-voice wavetable       |
|                  | engine.                                             |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| External Sound   | @{b}VU Mode@{ub}                                             |
|                  | You can either turn off or adjust the sensitivity   |
|                  | of the external VU meter by clicking and cycling    |
|                  | through the options.                                |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}TOSLINK@{ub}                                             |
|                  | By clicking on the cycle gadget, you can set the    |
|                  | audio sample rate of the TOSLINK output. Available  |
|                  | options are 48 kHz, 96 kHz and 192 kHz.             |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| Levels           | Realtime display of the stereo audio signal levels. |
|                  | By clicking on the cycle gadget below, you can      |
|                  | control the display's update rate.                  |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| Save /           | Save, Use or Reset the Mixer settings to their      |
| Use /            | default values. The settings are automatically      |
| Reset            | loaded when the Amiga is started or reset.          |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+

@{" 3.3   FPGA Flash Tool               " LINK flash1}
@{" 3.3.1 Available Options             " LINK flash2}

@ENDNODE

@NODE flash1 "Software Tools - FPGA Flash Tool"
@TOC contents
@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK sw_inst20}
@{" 3.1   Installation                  " LINK sw_inst20}
