@NODE aguslib "amigus.library"
@TOC contents
@{" 3     Software Tools                " LINK mixer1}
@{" 4     amigus.library                " LINK aguslib}

amigus.library is the basis of the tools or drivers will work without it.
It only adds a tiny abstraction layer on top of the AmiGUS cards enabling
developers to transparently write software for AmiGUS and AmiGUS mini at
the same point in time without worrying about the underlying
Zorro or PCMCIA interfaces to the cards. 

Therefore, it provides detection, reservation, and interrupt handling for
all variants of the AmiGUS cards. Instead of using card.resource,
expansion.library, or banging Gary directly, using the amigus.library
abstracts the hardware so developers can use the AmiGUS chip's
RBI (Register Banging Interface) without impacting the AmiGUS's variant.

@{" 4.1   amigus.library dev kit        " LINK aguslib_dev}
@ENDNODE

@NODE aguslib_dev "AmiGUS development RBI"
@TOC contents
@{" 4     amigus.library                " LINK aguslib}
@{" 4.1   amigus.library dev kit        " LINK aguslib_dev}

The AmiGUS developers' kit is not automatically installed, but it can be
found as amigus-dev.lha on 1.3 or 2.0+ AmiGUS-Tools disk (or .lha file)
in the "Base" folder. It includes ASM, C, E, and fd files. 

The process using amigus.library is pretty straight forward.

1) ----> AmiGUS_FindCard
         - Enumerates AmiGUS cards for you, providing information and
           base addresses for the RBI (Register Banging Interface).
2) +---> AmiGUS_ReserveCard
   |     - Reserves an AmiGUS card of your choice for you -
   |       or tells you if it is in use already.
   |       Do not use an AmiGUS you did not reserve successfully!
3) |+--> AmiGUS_InstallInterrupt
   ||    - Installs an interrupt handling routine for you.
4) ||+-> Calls your "LONG HandleInterrupt( APTR __regA0 data)" function
   ||    - Remember, the interrupt handler is called by C code, so you
   ||      will want to save/restore registers to/from stack at the
   ||      beginning/end if writing ASM.
5) |+--> AmiGUS_RemoveInterrupt
   |     - Removes your interrupt handler again
6) +---> AmiGUS_FreeCard
         - Gives up your AmiGUS reservation again.
           Do it - otherwise you will block other applications/drivers
           from using the AmiGUS
           (okay, maybe the users can "avail flush", but... com'on!)

That's it. Additional information on the library functions can be
found in the @{"AmiGUS github repository (9)" LINK refs}.

If you want the AmiGUS to do something, you need to use the
RBI (Register Banging Interface) additionally. For help, check out the
base tools, the driver implementations, and for a detailed
AmiGUS register map, please refer to the @{"AmiGUS github repository (10)" LINK refs}.

@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ahi "AHI Driver Framework"
@TOC contents
@{" 4     amigus.library                " LINK aguslib}
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}

AHI is a retargetable audio subsystem for Amiga-like operating systems,
originally developed by Martin Blom.
Not only have major audio applications, like SoundFX and DigiBooster,
adapted this standard, but many PC/Windows ports, Mac emulators, and even
some exclusive software implemented their audio functionality employing
AHI to allow use of sound cards for playback and recording.

Although there is @{"AHI 6.0 (2)" LINK refs} available for download, @{"AHI 4.18 (1)" LINK refs} is
assumed to be faster and therefore strongly recommended for use on
68k-based systems. You need to choose one of them and install it
following their respective manual(s).

Before configuring AHI for use with AmiGUS mini, please verify that
AHI is already working correctly. AHI comes with a Paula driver that
is sufficient for that purpose.

Please note:
  - AHI 4 requires AmigaOS 2.0 or better to work.
  - AHI 4 preferences editor shown below uses @{"MUI 3.8 (3)" LINK refs},
    the non-MUI version works in a similar fashion.
  - AHI 6 (not recommended) requires a 68020 processor or better.
  - Due to limitations of the AHI framework, AmiGUS's AHI driver is
    limited to a 64000 Hz mixing rate.

@{" 5.1   Using AHI with AmiGUS mini    " LINK ahi_amigus}
@{" 5.2   Selecting AHI Modes           " LINK ahi_modes}
@{" 5.3   Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI   " LINK ahi_config}
@{" 5.3.1 Available Options             " LINK ahi_options}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ahi_amigus "AHI Driver Framework - Using AHI with AmiGUS mini"
@TOC contents
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@{" 5.1   Using AHI with AmiGUS mini    " LINK ahi_amigus}

AHI should be your last resort for making software use your AmiGUS mini.
  - For playback of encoded music like MP3, FLAC, or OGG, please use MHI
    and let the AmiGUS mini hardware codec do the heavy lifting,
  - For tracker music, use a player directly supporting the
    AmiGUS mini wavetable engine, e.g. HippoPlayer 2.67 or later,
  - None of that works for your application? Here comes AHI.
    And only here.

@{" 5.2   Selecting AHI Modes           " LINK ahi_modes}
@{" 5.3   Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI   " LINK ahi_config}
@{" 5.3.1 Available Options             " LINK ahi_options}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ahi_modes "AHI Driver Framework - Selecting AHI Modes"
@TOC contents
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@{" 5.1   Using AHI with AmiGUS mini    " LINK ahi_amigus}
@{" 5.2   Selecting AHI Modes           " LINK ahi_modes}

AHI modes need to be selected to fit the purpose.

Example: Quake (clickboom) mixes audio in 16 bit, 11025 Hz.
  - If you select an 8-bit audio mode, you lose playback quality.
  - If you select a 24-bit audio mode, you waste precious CPU time.
  - If you select a sample rate less than 11025 Hz...
    well, you will not want to hear it anyway.
  - If you select more than 11025 Hz, you waste resources again.
  - If you do not at least select a multiple of 11025 Hz
    (like 1x, 2x, 4x), you cause AHI to re-render the audio stream,
    wasting even more CPU time.

But how do you determine the right AHI settings?

AmiGUS mini comes with an overwhelming number of choices.

Here are some guidelines:
  - If your game or application prints resolution ("...16-bit...") and
    sample rate ("...44.1 kHz..." or "...11025 Hz...") information
    somewhere, please use that information to adapt the AHI mode
    accordingly.
  - Otherwise, for PC ports, 16-bit resolution and 44100 Hz
    are usually fine.
  - "Mono" modes do not save a lot of CPU time, avoid them.
  - "Stereo" modes know "left" and "right" and nothing in between; fine
    for PC ports, they will mix as needed.
  - "Stereo++" modes allow positioning instruments between the "left" and
    "right" extremes, but these are useful only if the client software can
    use it. PC ports usually cannot, and you may waste resources here.
    DigiBooster for example can benefit from it.
  - "Fast..." modes take a bunch of shortcuts, saving a lot of CPU time,
    but potentially harming the audio quality, if you do not select the
    correct resolution and sample rate - really recommended if you are
    sure about both settings!
  - "HiFi..." modes are optimized for best possible quality and slower.
    Avoid them for games, if you are short on CPU time. Recommended for
    enjoying music as good as possible!

@{" 5.3   Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI   " LINK ahi_config}
@{" 5.3.1 Available Options             " LINK ahi_options}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ahi_config "AHI Driver Framework - Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI"
@TOC contents
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@{" 5.1   Using AHI with AmiGUS mini    " LINK ahi_amigus}
@{" 5.2   Selecting AHI Modes           " LINK ahi_modes}
@{" 5.3   Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI   " LINK ahi_config}

After all that theoretical foundation you are ready to select AmiGUS modes
using the AHI preferences editor usually installed to Sys:Prefs/AHI.
Please start it from there by double-clicking on its icon.

Recommendations:
  - Define a dedicated music mode, using all AmiGUS can deliver,
    "HiFi 24-bit stereo++" in 48000 Hz, and use it only for music.
  - Define some game modes, "Fast 16-bit stereo" in 11025 Hz,
    22050 Hz, and 44100 Hz each and use them for your favourite PC ports.

@{"AHI Prefs" system "MultiView ilbm/ahi0.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/ahi0.iff}

@{" 5.3.1 Available Options             " LINK ahi_options}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ahi_options "AHI Driver Framework - Available Options"
@TOC contents
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@{" 5.1   Using AHI with AmiGUS mini    " LINK ahi_amigus}
@{" 5.2   Selecting AHI Modes           " LINK ahi_modes}
@{" 5.3   Configuring AmiGUS mini AHI   " LINK ahi_config}
@{" 5.3.1 Available Options             " LINK ahi_options}

+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| Panel            | Description                                         |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| @{b}Left@{ub}             | @{b}AHI unit toggle@{ub}                                     |
|                  | @{b}(Music unit / Unit 0 / ...1 / ...2 / ...3)@{ub}          |
|                  | AHI supports defining up to five preset modes, the  |
|                  | default "Music unit" and the units 0, 1, 2, and 3.  |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}AHI Mode list@{ub}                                       |
|                  | We love Paula, but this is about AmiGUS mini.       |
|                  | So AmiGUS should be selected as the default         |
|                  | input/output device from the list.                  |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Mode details@{ub}                                        |
|                  | Informational display only, but if the text looks   |
|                  | broken or is empty, you know something is very      |
|                  | wrong.                                              |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| @{b}Right@{ub}            | @{b}Frequency@{ub}                                           |
| @{b}(Options)@{ub}        | Select the desired sample rate using the slider.    |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Volume@{ub}                                              |
|                  | The recommendation is to move the slider to the far |
|                  | right, +0.0dB and use the AmiGUS Mixer to adapt     |
|                  | the volume to your needs.                           |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Gain@{ub}                                                |
|                  | Sets the input gain - recommended to set to +0.0dB, |
|                  | too.                                                |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Input@{ub}                                               |
|                  | Selects the audio sources to record.                |
|                  | AmiGUS mini AHI driver allows recording Wave,       |
|                  | MHI / Codec, external sound, and                    |
|                  | "what you hear" / all sources for the time being.   |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+
| @{b}Buttons@{ub}          | @{b}Save@{ub}                                                |
|                  | Applies all changes, stores them permanently, so    |
|                  | they survive a reboot and closes the                |
|                  | AHI preferences editor.                             |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Use@{ub}                                                 |
|                  | Applies all changes and closes the AHI preferences  |
|                  | editor, without saving the settings permanently.    |
|                  | Can be used for testing.                            |
|                  |                                                     |
|                  | @{b}Cancel@{ub}                                              |
|                  | Discards all changes and closes the                 |
|                  | AHI preferences editor.                             |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+

@{" 6     MHI Driver Framework          " LINK mhi}
@ENDNODE

@NODE mhi "MHI Driver Framework"
@TOC contents
@{" 5     AHI Driver Framework          " LINK ahi}
@{" 6     MHI Driver Framework          " LINK mhi}

MHI is a standard for retargetable encoded audio for classic Amigas.
AmiGUS mini comes with the VLSI VS1063a audio codec chip supporting:
  - MP3 (MPEG 1 and 2 audio layer III, with CBR, VBR, and ABR)
  - MP2 (layer II)
  - MPEG4 / 2 AAC-LC (with PNS), HE-AAC v2 (Level 3) (SBR + PS)
  - Ogg Vorbis
  - FLAC
  - WMA 4.0/4.1/7/8/9 all profiles (5-384 kbps)
  - WAV (PCM, IMA ADPCM, G.711 (µ-law/A-law), G.722 ADPCM)
Unlike AHI, MHI comes without separate system-wide preferences. Every
application implements their own system of setting and selecting the
desired MHI compatible library for the installed hardware.
Per default, AmiGUS mini's MHI driver is installed to
Libs:mhi/mhiamigus.library on AmigaOS 2.0 and above.

@{" 6.1   Example: MHI in AmigaAmp      " LINK mhi_amigaamp}
@{" 6.2   Example: MHI in HippoPlayer   " LINK mhi_hippoplayer}
@ENDNODE

@NODE mhi_amigaamp "MHI Driver Framework - AmigaAmp"
@TOC contents
@{" 6     MHI Driver Framework          " LINK mhi}
@{" 6.1   Example: MHI in AmigaAmp      " LINK mhi_amigaamp}

As Thomas Wenzel not only created @{"AmigaAMP (4)" LINK refs} but has 
also been co-authoring MHI, integration of both works almost perfectly,
including equalizer functionality.
@{JCENTER}
@{"AmigaAmp Main" system "MultiView ilbm/amp0.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/amp0.iff}
@{JLEFT}
After starting AmigaAMP, open the preferences via the Project menu entry.
@{JCENTER}
@{"AmigaAmp Menu" system "MultiView ilbm/amp1.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/amp1.iff}
@{JLEFT}
In the preferences, navigate to the Decoder tab, tick the "MHI Support"
checkbox and click the file symbol next to "MHI Driver:".
@{JCENTER}
@{"AmigaAmp Prefs" system "MultiView ilbm/amp2.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/amp2.iff}
@{JLEFT}
Navigate to Libs:mhi and select the mhiamigus.library. Library names are
case-sensitive when loading and the more readable name had to be
sacrificed to support AmigaOS 1.3.
@{JCENTER}
@{"AmigaAmp MHI File Requester" system "MultiView ilbm/amp3.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/amp3.iff}
@{JLEFT}
Confirm with the "OK" button, close and confirm the preferences dialog
with the "Save" button. You may need to restart AmigaAMP immediately.
Add a few MP3 files to your playlist and enjoy!

@{" 6.2   Example: MHI in HippoPlayer   " LINK mhi_hippoplayer}
@ENDNODE

@NODE mhi_hippoplayer "MHI Driver Framework - HippoPlayer"
@TOC contents
@{" 6     MHI Driver Framework          " LINK mhi}
@{" 6.1   Example: MHI in AmigaAmp      " LINK mhi_amigaamp}
@{" 6.2   Example: MHI in HippoPlayer   " LINK mhi_hippoplayer}

@{"HippoPlayer (5, 6)" LINK refs} by K-P Koljonen supports AmiGUS mini's
wavetable engine natively for tracker module playback as of version 2.67
onwards.
@{JCENTER}
@{"HippoPlayer Main" system "MultiView ilbm/hippo0.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/hippo0.iff}
@{JLEFT}
Click the "Pr" button to open the preferences window.
@{JCENTER}
@{"HippoPlayer Prefs" system "MultiView ilbm/hippo1.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/hippo1.iff}
@{JLEFT}
Navigate to the "Play-2" tab, select the "Enable MHI" checkbox and push
the button right next to it, where it reads "mhiamigus" above.
@{JCENTER}
@{"HippoPlayer MHI File Requester" system "MultiView ilbm/hippo2.iff"}@{IMG ilbm/hippo2.iff}
@{JLEFT}
Navigate to the mhiamigus.library in Libs:MHI.
Select it, push the "OK" button, and save the preferences using the
"Save" button.
Add a few MP3 files to your playlist and enjoy!


@{" 7     Driver troubleshooting        " LINK drivers}
@ENDNODE

@NODE drivers "Driver troubleshooting"
@TOC contents
@{" 6     MHI Driver Framework          " LINK mhi}
@{" 7     Driver troubleshooting        " LINK drivers}

amigus.library, AHI and MHI drivers come with release and debug versions.
Never use the debug versions for normal operations - they may introduce
more crashes and are slower!

  - "...-SER_LOG" versions of the drivers log to the serial port of the
    Amiga. You may use Sashimi to redirect the output back into a
    Workbench window, but the speed of that debug output is limited,
    therefore the level of detail as well. A real null-modem cable can be
    used to get the output to another computer. Please find a link to a
    tested wiring at @{"(7)" LINK refs}.
  - "...-FILE_LOG" versions of the drivers log to a file on the Amiga. By
    default, this is "RAM:AmiGUS-?.log", but can be adapted on AmigaOS 2.0
    and above using
    "setenv AmiGUS-LOG-FILEPATH SYS:amigus_lib_log.txt", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-AHI-LOG-FILEPATH SYS:my_AHI_log.txt", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-MHI-LOG-FILEPATH SYS:another-Log-MHI.txt" respectively.
    Please be careful to not overwrite important files and don't be
    surprised if your drive wants to validate for a long time in
    case of crashes!
  - "...-MEM_LOG" debug versions of the drivers allocate a chunk of memory
    and leave their messages in there. This is as fast as it can be and as
    noisy as it can get; but it survives crashes well and will be requested
    in case of bug reports.
    "setenv AmiGUS-LOG-ADDRESS 1234560", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-AHI-LOG-ADDRESS 1234560", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-MHI-LOG-ADDRESS 1234560" sets the log address in case
    the automatic methods fail. Beware, the addresses are noted in decimal.
    The memory size can be adapted to fit your machine using
    "setenv AmiGUS-LOG-SIZE 512", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-AHI-LOG-SIZE 512", or
    "setenv AmiGUS-MHI-LOG-SIZE 512" with a decimal memory size.
    The memory address actually used is printed to the serial port on
    driver startup.
    Beware: this is just for allocation and protecting the resulting logs.
    The drivers do not stop producing more logs behind the end of that
    memory slab.

The tools explained below are part of the debug tools offered during
installation.
  - Even after a crash, the Get#?MemLog tools can retrieve the respective
    memory logs into files. Get#?MemLog accepts optional parameters, 
    try "help" for more information.
  - The "AmiGUSLibCheck" utility runs some basic tests of your
    AmiGUS setup through the amigus.library. If these fail, something
    is odd with your setup.
  - MHILibCheck tries loading the AmiGUS MHI driver and calls a method
    retrieving additional information from it. This is especially useful
    if the MHI driver is not loaded successfully in OS 1.3. If it does not
    work here, something is very wrong.
  - MHIplay is a command line player for MP3s and similar encoded files.
    While PlayMP3 directly talks to the hardware, this now tries using
    the MHI driver. If that works and another player does not work with
    the MHI driver correctly, it is likely the other player's fault.
    MHIplay requires two parameters, the driver and the file,
    e.g.
    > mhiplay libs:mhi/mhiamigus.library music:nena/99_red_balloons.mp3

We hope you will never need this information;
please be patient, we did our best!

@{" 8     Appendices                    " LINK credits}
@ENDNODE

